Lay out EPUB spine items lazily: only up to the pages asked for, resuming
later where the layout stopped. Plato lays out a whole spine item before it
can place a position in it or draw any of its pages; a MOBI converted to one
huge spine item ("Pan Tadeusz", 420 KB) costs 2 to 3 s on the PW4 for every open,
placement and font change, wherever the reader is in it.

The engine's block loop can now stop between two children of a block (never
inside a paragraph or a table) once enough complete pages exist after the
location asked for, and leaves one frame per nesting level: the next child,
the loop context and the rectangles so far. Resuming replays exactly the
operations a full layout performs (the work done before the stop is not
repeated), so page breaks are identical: checked by comparing every page
start, 20 placements in fresh documents and rendered pages of 38 books
against an unpatched build.

EpubDocument keeps a partly laid out spine item (its document, styles and
layout state) next to its complete pages in the page cache, and continues it
when a location past them is asked for: 2 pages ahead on the first layout,
8 ahead when continuing. Going back into a previous spine item still lays it
out whole (its last page is needed). HtmlDocument is unchanged.

